@page "/settings/profile"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Components
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Identity
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Photos
@using DLR.Core.Display
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject AuthState Auth
@inject RiderAvatars Avatars

@*
	Two optional fields and a marker colour (§7.3, §16.3) — everything on this screen is an
	account setting that travels to the server.

	The display name was a third. It is gone from the screen, not from the account: every name a
	traveller reads — map pin, member list, marker author — has always been the login name (§7.2),
	so the field only offered a second name that nothing rendered. The column, the contract and the
	sharing switch all stay where they are for a later use, and this page keeps round-tripping the
	stored values untouched (see _displayName) because PUT /me/profile replaces the whole profile —
	a save that left them out would quietly erase what the screen no longer shows.

	The home private area used to sit at the bottom of it and now lives on the Location screen
	(§10.1). It is stored on the account like the fields above — but it is not one of them: it is
	not a fact other riders may be shown, it is a gate on the receiver, and it belongs beside the
	receiver it gates and the recording it filters. It is deliberately not part of this form's
	PUT /me/profile either, for the reason spelled out two paragraphs up: this page replaces the
	whole profile on every save, and a privacy control must not be erasable by a client that has
	not been taught about it. It has its own endpoints.
*@

<PageTitle>Profile — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

<div class="page page-medium form-page profile">
	<PageNav Title="Profile" BackHref="/settings" BackLabel="Back to settings" />

	<p class="lead">
		Two optional fields. Each has a switch, off unless you turn it on — a shared value
		is visible <em>only</em> to travellers in a group adventure with you, and access ends the
		moment that adventure ends.
	</p>

	@if (_profile is null)
	{
		<p class="empty">Loading…</p>
	}
	else
	{
		@*
			Outside the form, and it saves on its own. Everything below goes up as one whole-profile
			PUT; the photograph has its own endpoint so that a client which has not been taught
			about it cannot clear it by saving the rest (§7.3) — and putting it inside a form whose
			Save button does not save it would be the confusing half of that decision.
		*@
		<fieldset class="photo">
			<legend>Photo</legend>

			<p class="note">
				Optional. Add one and it appears as a small circle beside your name everywhere
				other travellers read it — member lists, adventure chat, markers you place, routes
				you share. There is no switch: adding it is how you share it, and Remove is how
				you stop.
			</p>

			<div class="photo-row">
				@if (_pickedPhoto is not null)
				{
					<span class="picked-note"><em>@_pickedPhoto.FileName — @(_pickedPhoto.Bytes.Length / 1024) KB</em></span>
				}
				else if (Auth.UserName is { Length: > 0 } me)
				{
					@* The live component rather than a bare <img>, so what the rider is looking at
					   is exactly what everybody else will see — same cache, same size, same crop. *@
					<RiderAvatar UserName="@me" Size="RiderAvatar.AvatarSize.Detail" />
				}

				<div class="photo-actions">
					<InputFile OnChange="OnPhotoSelectedAsync" accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/heic,image/webp" />

					@if (_pickedPhoto is not null)
					{
						<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@_savingPhoto" @onclick="SavePhotoAsync">
							@(_savingPhoto ? "Uploading…" : "Use this photo")
						</button>
						<button type="button" disabled="@_savingPhoto" @onclick="() => _pickedPhoto = null">Cancel</button>
					}
					else if (_profile.AvatarPhotoId is not null)
					{
						<button type="button" class="danger" disabled="@_savingPhoto" @onclick="RemovePhotoAsync">Remove</button>
					}
				</div>
			</div>

			<p class="note">
				The server re-encodes every photo and strips its metadata — an EXIF GPS tag from
				your driveway will not travel with it.
			</p>

			@if (_photoStatus is not null)
			{
				<p class="status">@_photoStatus</p>
			}
		</fieldset>

		<form @onsubmit="SaveAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
			<fieldset>
				<legend>Phone number</legend>
				<input @bind="_phoneNumber" type="tel" placeholder="Any digits, never verified" />
				<label class="switch">
					<input type="checkbox" @bind="_sharePhoneNumber" />
					Share with travellers in my group adventures
				</label>
				<p class="note">
					Not verified and never used for account recovery. One-tap-to-call
					for mates on an adventure, and nothing else.
				</p>
			</fieldset>

			<fieldset>
				<legend>Email</legend>
				@if (_profile.Email is null)
				{
					<p><em>No email on file.</em>
						<a href="/settings/account#email">Add one to enable password recovery.</a>
					</p>
				}
				else
				{
					<p>
						<strong>@_profile.Email</strong>
						@if (_profile.EmailConfirmed)
						{
							<span class="badge good">confirmed</span>
						}
						else
						{
							<span class="badge bad">unconfirmed</span>
						}
					</p>
					<label class="switch">
						<input type="checkbox" @bind="_shareEmail" disabled="@(!_profile.EmailConfirmed)" />
						Share with travellers in my group adventures
					</label>
					<p class="note">
						Sharing an email exposes your recovery address. Turning it off does
						not delete it — it stays your recovery address either way.
						<a href="/settings/account#email">Change it, or resend the confirmation link.</a>
					</p>
				}
			</fieldset>

			@*
				§16.3. Only the background is chosen: the text and the border are whichever of
				black or white reads on it, so a colour that would leave someone invisible on a
				ride map cannot be picked in the first place.
			*@
			<fieldset class="marker-colour">
				<legend>Map marker colour</legend>

				<p class="note">
					How you appear on a group adventure's live map. The arrow points the way you are
					going; it turns into a red dot when you stop. Everyone on the adventure sees this
					colour — that is the whole point of it.
				</p>

				<p class="marker-preview">
					<span class="pill" style="background: @MarkerBackground; color: @MarkerForeground; border-color: @MarkerForeground;">
						<span class="dot" style="border-color: @MarkerForeground;"></span>
						<span class="who">@MarkerLabel</span>
					</span>
				</p>

				<div class="swatches" role="group" aria-label="Marker colour">
					@foreach (string option in MarkerColours.Palette)
					{
						bool chosen = string.Equals(option, MarkerBackground, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

						<button type="button" class="swatch @(chosen ? "chosen" : null)"
								style="background: @option"
								aria-pressed="@(chosen ? "true" : "false")"
								aria-label="@option"
								title="@option"
								@onclick="() => _markerColour = option"></button>
					}
				</div>

				<label class="custom-colour">
					Any other colour
					<input type="color" value="@MarkerBackground" @onchange="OnCustomColourChosen" />
				</label>
			</fieldset>

			<button type="submit" class="primary" disabled="@_saving">
				@(_saving ? "Saving…" : "Save")
			</button>

			@if (_status is not null)
			{
				<p class="status">@_status</p>
			}
		</form>
	}

	<p class="note">
		Looking for the home private area? It has moved to
		<a href="/settings/location">Location</a>, beside the GPS it switches off. It is saved with
		your account, so it follows you to a new phone — and it is still shown to nobody else.
	</p>
</div>

@code {
	private OwnProfile? _profile;

	/// <summary>
	/// Read on load and sent straight back, with nothing on the screen that changes them. The
	/// display name has no editor any more (see the note at the top), but the update is a
	/// whole-profile replace — a save that omitted these would clear a stored name and the
	/// switch that goes with it.
	/// </summary>
	private string? _displayName;

	/// <inheritdoc cref="_displayName" />
	private bool _shareDisplayName;

	private string? _phoneNumber;
	private bool _sharePhoneNumber;
	private bool _shareEmail;
	private bool _saving;
	private string? _status;

	/// <summary>An image chosen but not yet uploaded. See OnPhotoSelectedAsync.</summary>
	private PickedPhoto? _pickedPhoto;

	/// <summary>Guards the photo buttons on their own — they save separately from the form below.</summary>
	private bool _savingPhoto;

	/// <summary>What the photo section reports, kept apart from the form's own status line.</summary>
	private string? _photoStatus;

	/// <summary>The largest image the photo endpoint will take (§16.4), checked at the keyboard.</summary>
	private const long MaxPhotoBytes = 12 * 1024 * 1024;

	/// <summary>
	/// The marker background being composed, always a <c>#rrggbb</c> string (§16.3). Held
	/// normalised rather than as the raw stored value so the swatch comparison and the preview do
	/// not each have to decide what an account with nothing stored looks like.
	/// </summary>
	private string _markerColour = MarkerColours.Default;

	/// <summary>The chosen background, as both the preview and the swatch comparison read it.</summary>
	private string MarkerBackground => MarkerColours.Or(_markerColour);

	/// <summary>Black or white — the ink the map will draw the name and the border in.</summary>
	private string MarkerForeground => MarkerColours.ForegroundFor(_markerColour);

	/// <summary>
	/// What the preview pill says. The username, because that — never the display name — is what
	/// a map pin carries (§7.2). "You" while the session is still warming up.
	/// </summary>
	private string MarkerLabel => RiderMarker.Label(Auth.UserName ?? "You");

	protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
	{
		_profile = await Api.GetProfileAsync();
		_displayName = _profile.DisplayName;
		_phoneNumber = _profile.PhoneNumber;
		_shareDisplayName = _profile.ShareDisplayName;
		_sharePhoneNumber = _profile.SharePhoneNumber;
		_shareEmail = _profile.ShareEmail;
		_markerColour = MarkerColours.Or(_profile.MarkerColour);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The colour well's answer. Browsers hand back <c>#rrggbb</c>, and anything else is dropped
	/// rather than sent — the server would refuse it, and a save that fails over a control the
	/// rider cannot see the state of is a save nobody can fix.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnCustomColourChosen(ChangeEventArgs args)
	{
		if (args.Value is string chosen && HexColour.IsHex(chosen))
		{
			_markerColour = chosen.ToLowerInvariant();
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Reads the chosen image into memory. Nothing is uploaded until <em>Use this photo</em> —
	/// cancelling then leaves nothing on the server for the §7.11 sweep to find.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task OnPhotoSelectedAsync(InputFileChangeEventArgs args)
	{
		IBrowserFile file = args.File;
		_photoStatus = null;

		if (file.Size > MaxPhotoBytes)
		{
			// Said at the keyboard rather than after a 413 from the far end of a phone connection,
			// which is the case this check exists for.
			_photoStatus = "That photo is larger than 12 MB — the server would refuse it.";
			return;
		}

		try
		{
			await using Stream stream = file.OpenReadStream(MaxPhotoBytes);
			using MemoryStream memory = new();
			await stream.CopyToAsync(memory);

			_pickedPhoto = new PickedPhoto(memory.ToArray(), file.ContentType, file.Name);
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_photoStatus = $"Could not read that photo: {exception.Message}";
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Uploads the picked image and points the account at it.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	/// Two calls rather than one, because a photo is a standalone resource (§16.4). The upload
	/// going through and the account not being updated leaves an orphan the nightly sweep
	/// collects — a far better failure than an account pointing at bytes that never landed.
	/// </remarks>
	private async Task SavePhotoAsync()
	{
		if (_pickedPhoto is not { } picked) return;

		_savingPhoto = true;
		_photoStatus = null;

		try
		{
			using MemoryStream memory = new(picked.Bytes);

			PhotoUploaded uploaded = await Api.UploadPhotoAsync(memory, picked.ContentType, picked.FileName);

			_profile = await Api.SetAvatarAsync(new SetAvatarRequest(uploaded.PhotoId));
			_pickedPhoto = null;

			// Every avatar of this rider currently on screen re-resolves — including the preview
			// two lines above, which is the same component reading the same cache.
			Avatars.Forget(Auth.UserName);

			_photoStatus = "Photo saved.";
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_photoStatus = $"Could not save that photo: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_savingPhoto = false;
		}
	}

	private async Task RemovePhotoAsync()
	{
		_savingPhoto = true;
		_photoStatus = null;

		try
		{
			_profile = await Api.ClearAvatarAsync();

			Avatars.Forget(Auth.UserName);

			_photoStatus = "Photo removed. Your name appears on its own again.";
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_photoStatus = $"Could not remove it: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_savingPhoto = false;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>An image read off the device and waiting for a deliberate press.</summary>
	private sealed record PickedPhoto(byte[] Bytes, string ContentType, string FileName);

	private async Task SaveAsync()
	{
		_saving = true;
		_status = null;
		try
		{
			await Api.UpdateProfileAsync(new UpdateProfileRequest(
				// Unchanged since load, and on the wire for exactly that reason — see _displayName.
				DisplayName: string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_displayName) ? null : _displayName.Trim(),
				PhoneNumber: string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_phoneNumber) ? null : _phoneNumber.Trim(),
				ShareDisplayName: _shareDisplayName,
				SharePhoneNumber: _sharePhoneNumber,
				ShareEmail: _shareEmail,
				MarkerColour: MarkerBackground));
			_status = "Saved.";
			_profile = await Api.GetProfileAsync();
			_markerColour = MarkerColours.Or(_profile.MarkerColour);
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_status = $"Save failed: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_saving = false;
		}
	}
}

<style>
	.profile .switch { margin-top: 0.5rem; }

	.profile .photo {
		border: 0;
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
		padding: 0;
		min-width: 0; /* fieldset defaults to min-content, which stops the row wrapping */
	}

		.profile .photo legend {
			padding: 0;
		}

	.profile .photo-row {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 1rem;
		margin: 0.75rem 0;
	}

	.profile .photo-actions {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
	}
	.profile .badge.good { background: #d1fae5; color: #196c2e; }
	.profile .badge.bad { background: #fef3c730; color: #92400e; }
	.profile .status { margin-top: 1rem; }

	/* The preview is the marker the map draws, in HTML: a pill with a rounded end, the rider's
	   colour behind it and the contrasting ink on top. Not pixel-identical to the Skia version
	   and not trying to be — what it has to get right is the pairing. */
	.profile .marker-preview {
		margin: 0.75rem 0;
	}

		.profile .marker-preview .pill {
			display: inline-flex;
			align-items: center;
			gap: 0.4rem;
			padding: 0.2rem 0.7rem 0.2rem 0.2rem;
			border: 1px solid;
			border-radius: 999px;
			font-weight: 600;
			line-height: 1.2;
		}

		.profile .marker-preview .dot {
			width: 0.85rem;
			height: 0.85rem;
			border: 1px solid;
			border-radius: 50%;
			/* The travelling blue. The map turns it red below walking pace; the preview shows the
			   moving state because that is the one a rider spends a ride looking at. */
			background: #2563eb;
		}

	.profile .swatches {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

		.profile .swatches .swatch {
			/* 44 px square: the same gloved-thumb target the rest of the app uses. */
			width: 44px;
			height: 44px;
			padding: 0;
			border: 2px solid var(--dlr-border);
			border-radius: 8px;
			cursor: pointer;
		}

		.profile .swatches .swatch.chosen {
			/* Outline rather than a tick: a tick would have to be black or white and would
			   disappear into whichever end of the palette it was drawn over. */
			border-color: var(--dlr-fg);
			box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--dlr-surface) inset;
		}

	.profile .custom-colour {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
	}

</style>
